Z||000004||EdsB52||08-02-2000||11:16 PM||||Come on Shame, let's not start a pissin' contest over something as semantic as the difference between an engine and a motor. Especially since the original poster of this topic is right and you're wrong. I'm sure we all know that conventionally, a motor is considered something that converts electrical energy into mechanical motion, and an engine is something that produces mechanical motion through internal combustion of fuel. Why don't you look in Webster's as to the definition of these two words, I think you'll be surprised.<P>Webster's defines a motor as either; <P>1. A comparatively small engine, esp. an internal combustion engine, as in an automobile.<P>2. A machine that converts electrical energy into mechanical energy.<P>For instance, since an automobile has a motor, we refer to them as 'motor vehicles.' Wouldn't a motor vehicle be an electric car if the definitions were as you suggest them to be?<P>Either way, I've been an Aviation Mechanic for twenty years, and an FAA licensed inspector for eight. And I still refer to reciprocal engines, motors, and turbine engines as motors and have never received complaints of ambiguity. Thanks to you, I now realize that I've been grammatically correct all along!||||reg||1
Z||000005||88 Fiero GT Turbo||08-02-2000||11:20 PM||||Thanks from the new guy<P>Later,<BR>Jack||||reg||1
Z||000006||EdsB52||08-02-2000||11:32 PM||||Sure thing bro. Hey Jack, I'm in the same boat as you with my 87GT. The motor isn't gone, but is going and I have some time and money freed up to rebuild my MOTOR. I hope some members post some replies to your question, as I would like to increase HP with little more effort. I think the 3.1 conversion might be just the changing of the stroke and heads (just a guess). Please, other members, allow for my naivety and regurgitate what you guys know like the back of your hand. Thanks, Ed.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Old Lar||08-03-2000||06:58 PM||||Swapping out new engines (motors) can be pricy if you don't do the work yourself.<P>I had a remanufactured 3.1 L engine from Pisa $2200 plus install at Mr Goodwrench noless for $1200.<P>I'm having a new 3.4L put in probably in the neighborhood of $4200 with installation.<P>So if you rebuild an engine parts $500-1000 plus your time and your skills and tools to do it right.  Now if you do it yourself you can do all the mods you want.<P>When you read all the posts about the engines and the mods, swaps and fitting the stuff together and then it doesn't work, you'd have to be a real cam jammer to even want to do the work yourself.  I take the deep pocket route, because I want the car to work reliably.  I let the people who have done this before many times do the work.<P>||||reg||1
