Z||000011||peterh||08-04-2000||10:13 AM||||I used to rebuild motors, but now I would defintely buy one of the 3.4 crate motors for $1700. It would save a lot of time and money. To properly rebuild a motor requires machine shop work (read dollars) which builds up fast. I understand there is some modifications to use this engine in our fieros but it is still worth it.<BR>Just my opinion.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000012||Black88GT||08-04-2000||02:43 PM||||$4,200 with installation? Somebody is taking you for a ride.  Look for a local mechanic or a small time operation.  I bought a 3.4 from a 95 Firebird with 7k miles on it for $850.  Then I bought the starter hole plate for $150.  Add $400 for installation, and you have $1,400.  Now of course I am replacing/upgrading a bunch of other stuff too, but if your doing strictly stock replacement and can use all of your old Fiero stuff, you can do it for under $1,500. I talked to the head technician at a local GM dealership and he said the 3.4 crate engine wasn't even worth buying for that much money.  When all is said and done I will have about 2,000 into mine, that's including all the parts I replaced/upgraded and including installation. (see reliability post for parts list)<P>jeff<BR>||||reg||11
Z||000013||elfiero||08-04-2000||03:54 PM||||REMEBER GUYS,NO MATTER WHAT THEY SAY,BIGGER<BR>IS BETTER AND SIZE DOES MATTER.ALSO IT'S<BR>QUANTITY OVER QLUALITY.THERES NO REPLACEMENT<BR>FOR DISPLACEMENT.GET THE IDEA?...THIS ALSO<BR>APPLYS TO CAR ENGINES||||reg||10
Z||000014||chris west||08-04-2000||08:39 PM||||   Whats up guys this is Will here at West Coast Fiero here. You r right that $4,200 is to much to spend on a basic swap over but if it was a hi-po set up then that would b a diffrent story all together. We have had some swaps go well past $10,000 to get the wanted performance out of the car. $2,000 is what a good plain swap should cost depending of the condition of the fiero that it is being put in to I.E. manifolds, mounts, electrical, and  ect. If you would like to get some more info u can look us up on the web at <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A>  or call at 310 305 4111 thanks for your time i hope that this helps.||||reg||6
Z||000015||88formula||08-04-2000||10:01 PM||||88 Fiero GT Turbo to answer your question there are three engines that belong to the 60-degree family that will bolt up to the fiero. The first is the 2.8-liter you have in your car now which has a 3.5" bore and 2.99" stroke. The second is a 3.1 with a 3.5" bore and 3.31" stroke. The 3.1 is just a 2.8 with a longer stroke crankshaft. The third is the 3.4 with a 3.620" bore and 3.31" stroke. The 3.4 has the same crank as the 3.1 but the block is a little beefier to accept the larger bore. <P>You have two options for increasing displacement. One you can rebuild you 2.8 and put a stroker crankshaft in from a 90-91 3.1Camaro engine and make 3.1-liters or 3.2 with a .040" overbore. The only other thing you have to change is the pistons (Silvolite 1486 pistons 8.8 compression), the heads are the same. The 3.31” stroke crankshaft will give you gobs of low-end torque. Your other option is to buy a used 3.4 engine from a 93-95 Camaro or get the GM crate engine. You will have to relocate the starter to the other side by drilling new starter holes and modify the front engine mount bracket and AC bracket.  If you get a used low mileage Camaro engine you will also get the injectors that come with it which are bigger than the one’s on your 2.8 engine. The 3.4 engine will give you the most power and torque and I think is also the cheapest to do. Ed Parks is an expert on this swap because he dose them all the time in his shop. <A HREF="http://www.angelfire.com/on/fierofactory/start.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.angelfire.com/on/fierofactory/start.html</A>  <P>I forgot to tell you that the oiling system on the 3.4 engine is far superior to the 2.8 oiling system. I paid $550 for my 3.4 engine which came from a 93 Firebird. <BR><p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 08-04-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000016||Old Lar||08-04-2000||10:08 PM||||Chris/Will I hate to be picky, but your website prices for an install of a 3800/3800s/c is nearly $5000 without the engine.<P>I'm getting a NEW 3.4 engine AND installation for $4200.<P>West Coast Fiero has a great reputation for excellent work.  If I were located in California, you would be a vendor of choice. <P>There are too many hack mechanics out there that say they can do a job, but I have found that you get what you pay for.  Cheap ain't they way to go for transportation.||||reg||1
Z||000017||EdsB52||08-05-2000||05:23 PM||||elfiero wrote;<P>"REMEBER GUYS,NO MATTER WHAT THEY SAY,BIGGER<BR>IS BETTER AND SIZE DOES MATTER.ALSO IT'S<BR>QUANTITY OVER QLUALITY.THERES NO REPLACEMENT<BR>FOR DISPLACEMENT.GET THE IDEA?...THIS ALSO<BR>APPLYS TO CAR ENGINES."<P>With the exception of drag racing or tractor-trailer applications, I totally disagree with that philosophy.<P>In road racing, weight and weight distribution are obviously highly important. The Acura NSX pushes that baby at 170mph with an all aluminum v6 - 3000cc. I don't know the actual tourque or HP output.<P>In aviation, the philosophy is "less is more." That's one reason why your basic Lycoming engine for a four seat Cessna 172, a Lycoming IO-360, costs $18,000 new. This engine only puts out about 180 hp, but doesn't weight very much and has extreme reliability attributes. I talked to an engineer that told me it would require 3-4 times the total hp output to gain 50 mph, given the added weight associated with larger engines, hence airframes and relocated CG's. He was engineering an engine / airframe combo that was going to be an air racer that was supposed to travel at 350 -400 mph on 100hp (Lycoming 0-200). The thing made a BD-5 look big, but less is more with that application.<P>Boats: Don't have a clue, but I bet weight does matter.<P>I would say that if you limit the scope of that statement to engines in drag cars, or engines sitting on dynomomiters, that you would be right on with "bigger is better." But in real life applications, a myriad of other attributes must be considered.||||reg||1
Z||000018||88 Fiero GT Turbo||08-05-2000||05:35 PM||||Ok guys I think we got away from the original question in hand.  I am looking for your opinion on what the better motor(engine) to put in my car.<P><BR>Later,<BR>Jack||||reg||1
Z||000019||Old Lar||08-05-2000||06:20 PM||||Jack:  All I can suggest is you get what you pay for.  A remaunrfactured 2.8 is one option.  The 3.1 swap is a potential as is a 3.4 L.  If you want more power go for the 3.1 or 3.4.  The 3.1 puts out maybe 150 HP, the 3.4 maybe 170 HP. With these two swaps you can use the Fiero ECM and wiring harnesses. Find a reliable mechanic.  The 3.1 is probably a good swap.  You have many choices on where to get replacement engines ATK or Jasper make remanufactured engines and Joe's Junkyard has used engines.  Unless you get a used Fiero engine (2.8), like a Camaro 2.8, there will be additional work in putting it into a Fiero.  If you have deep pockets you can get a 3800 / 3800 S/C engine.<P>AS you can see by the variety of answers to your original post, there are many diverse suggestions.  Let your pocket be your guide, but you can expect to pay $1000 on up for a replacemet.||||reg||1
Z||000020||terryk||08-05-2000||08:33 PM||||Will,<P>On the West Coast swaps, does the price include BAR re-certification?||||reg||1
Z||000021||-=shame=-||08-06-2000||10:16 AM||||OK, I would honestly look into a rebuild, they're less expensive on average (you could go all out and have it rebored and the compression changed). Plus, you won't have to worry so much about mounting every little thing onto a new engine AC etc. A rebuild I've looked into will run about $800 (thats not including dropping (or lifting) the engine).||||reg||1
Z||000022||rockcrawl||08-06-2000||10:48 AM||||Hey, Jon here. If you decide to go with a rebuilt engine, be carefull which rebuilder you chose. I've had two that lasted less than 20,000 miles. The first was a Ford 302 from Recon that ended up with a hole in a piston, the second was a Fiero 2.8 that ended up with a seized wrist pin at 12,000 miles. Needless to say, I'll never buy another rebuilt.<P>As stated earlier, the 3.4 has a superior oiling system, which is the downfall of the 2.8/3.1. For that reason, and also the increased power, I would recomend the 3.4. Personally I think the 2.8 is not a very good engine. <P>EdsB52, In the case of the 2.8, 3.1, 3.4, I'll have to agree with elfiero that bigger is better, because as displacement is increased, weight is not (much anyway).<P><P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'86 GT 3800 (for sale)<BR>'87 GT (noisey 2.8)<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)||||reg||1
Z||000023||EdsB52||08-06-2000||02:31 PM||||Ya rockcrawl, in the case of the narrow scope between the 2.8, 3.1, and 3.4 I do find the weight differences to be negligible. What is the difference between the 2.5 and 2.8? It feels like 300 pounds when you throw the car into a corner at 90mph, at the edge of breaking tire adhesion. In a straight line, The 2.8 feels lighter. I think the author of a statement like that should qualify it, or at least limit it before making that assertion. If a naive person took that statement at face value, he'd be throwing in a 454 (which I've seen pics of) and be popping wheelies down the road. The only problem would be cornering and maybe braking. I shudder when I hear the adage "there's no replacement for displacement." Is life that linear that we think along a continuum? Every time I think that mentality has gone away, it resurfaces somewhere. Maybe this club is filled with motor-heads, which are cool, but not condusive to roadracing. And I do believe people roadrace Fieros, right(don't want to be accused of being off-topic)? That must be how you guys keep your cars so damn nice, you never push 'em sideways!<P>I totally agree with you about not buying a rebuilt motor though. It's kinda like a box of chocolates, huh? Go with a crate, or better yet, do it yourself.||||reg||1
Z||000024||chris west||08-08-2000||01:51 PM||||   Hey guys its Will again the reason for the high cost is its not just a simple drop and go swap. Some hack guys that just drop them in and make things kind of work, but thats not what we like to do here and thats why our cars that we build are all prize winners as seen at the last show where we took top prize in all but too classes and second in the ones that we didnt. On the web site it talks about all of the aspects of the swap i.e. water inlet, brakets, and motor mounts. Some of the items on the list you dont have to do but we recomend it for you to get all of the power and exitment the you want. <BR>   To get the the second question our motors are not BAR certified motors as for the 3.4 swap we use low mile firebird motors and go through them to insure that they are in good shape. If the quality of the motor is a big factor as in you dont want a used motor. Then we would advise a crate motor from GM. I hope this answers some questions and maybe adds some more if any of you would like to ask some more you can call or post them here thanks.<P> Will West Coast Fiero||||reg||4
